import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Where the Mozambican records are"
  subtitle="A survey done on 14 September 2026 — searched, not assumed"
  lede="Ivone was born at Lourenço Marques on 4 March 1924 and this archive does not know her parents' names. This page is the map of where that answer is kept, including the places it is definitively not.">

  <h2>First, the negative — FamilySearch has nothing</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      The catalogue is the authoritative list of everything FamilySearch has ever filmed.
      <strong>Place = Mozambique returns 51 results</strong>, and the subject headings are Biography,
      Colonization, Description and travel, Ethnology, Gazetteers, History, Maps, Medical records,
      Military history, Names, Politics, Social life.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>No “Civil registration”. No “Church records”.</strong> Those are the headings
      FamilySearch always uses where it holds such things. It holds none for Mozambique.
      <span class="tag doc">Tested</span>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Consequence:</strong> no amount of searching FamilySearch will ever find Ivone's birth.
      That avenue is closed, and knowing it is closed is worth as much as a positive result.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lourenço Marques itself returns eleven items. One is a church record — <em>Parish registers
    1895–1940, <strong>Methodist</strong> Church</em>. The family were Catholic; wrong denomination.
    One other is worth a journey: the <strong>Lourenço Marques telephone directory for 1951</strong>,
    film 795898 item 6, image group DGS 9010130, 1,318 images. It is digitised but
    <strong>padlocked</strong> even to a signed-in user, which in that interface means it can be
    viewed only at a FamilySearch Center or affiliate library. A 1951 phone book of the colonial
    capital would list Figueiredo and Mota households, with addresses.
  </p>

  <h2>The Maputo archive holds her birth, and takes requests online</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique <span class="tag doc">The one that matters</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>The AHM issues birth, marriage and death certificates from the civil registration books
      it holds for 1865–1934.</strong> Ivone was born on 4 March 1924. She is inside the range.
    </p>
    <dl class="kv">
      <dt>Request portal</dt><dd><a href="https://documentos.uem.mz/#/front/index">documentos.uem.mz</a></dd>
      <dt>Email</dt><dd>ahm@uem.mz</dd>
      <dt>Address</dt><dd>Travessa do Varietá nº 58, C.P. 2033, Maputo</dd>
      <dt>Telephone</dt><dd>+258 21 323428</dd>
      <dt>Open</dt><dd>Monday to Friday, 07:30–15:30</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="small">
      A Portuguese birth registration names the child's parents, and commonly the grandparents too.
      One document would answer the question this archive has been circling all day.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Henrique falls outside it.</strong> Born 11 March 1941, he is past the AHM's 1934 cut-off;
    post-1934 colonial civil registration went to the <strong>Conservatória dos Registos Centrais</strong>
    in Lisbon. His birth record names his father — the <a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Pinto da Rocha</a>
    the family record cannot identify.
  </p>

  <h2>Lisbon, and a digitisation that finished five months ago</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Arquivo Histórico Ultramarino</strong> holds the metropolitan half of colonial
    administration, and it has just been opened up: <strong>176,413 images in March 2026</strong> and
    <strong>155,416 more in April</strong>, including the <em>Governo Geral de Moçambique</em> fond.
    Nobody has worked any of it for this family.
  </p>
  <p>
    More precisely useful is the series <strong>PT/AHU/MU-DGAPC/RNPAC</strong> — individual files on
    people going out to the colonies and coming back, roughly <strong>1933 to 1949</strong>, digitised
    and searchable by personal name. It takes two shapes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>“Processo do colono / da colona [name]”</em> — a personal file.</li>
    <li><em>“Lista de Colonos e <strong>Repatriados</strong> a bordo: […]”</em> — passenger lists naming everyone aboard.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Test searches pulled real Figueiredos straight out of it: a colonist file for João Henriques de
    Figueiredo in 1937, one for Margarida Duarte do Nascimento Figueiredo in 1941, nineteen images for
    José dos Prazeres Figueiredo in 1938, and a Leopoldina da Conceição Dias de Figueiredo appearing on
    two separate colonist-and-repatriate passenger lists in 1939 and 1940.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      Note the word <strong>Repatriados</strong>. Ivone's parents went out to Mozambique before 1924,
      which is before this series begins — but the series catches people coming <em>back</em>, and
      <strong>Ivone died in Porto</strong>. If the family returned to Portugal between 1933 and 1949,
      they are in here. <span class="tag inf">Untried</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Digitarq searches all of it, and it works</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://digitarq.arquivos.pt">digitarq.arquivos.pt</a> covers the Torre do Tombo, the
    Arquivo Histórico Ultramarino and every Portuguese district archive. A search on
    <strong>“Mota Figueiredo”</strong> returns <strong>223 results</strong>. Among them:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Archive</th><th>Date</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Passport register of Arménio de Figueiredo and Maria Sofia <strong>Mota de Figueiredo</strong></td><td>Arquivo Distrital de <strong>Aveiro</strong></td><td>1964</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Marriage of Paulo Serafim de Almeida Figueiredo and Cândida <strong>da Mota</strong></td><td>Arquivo Distrital do <strong>Porto</strong></td><td>1898</td></tr>
      <tr><td>João <strong>Mota Figueiredo</strong></td><td>Torre do Tombo</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    A <strong>Mota-de-Figueiredo family demonstrably exists</strong>, and it appears in Aveiro. Nothing
    yet connects it to Ivone — but the district <strong>passport registers are indexed by personal name
    and digitised</strong>, and anyone leaving Portugal for the colonies needed one. That is the most
    promising untried search in this survey, and the two districts to start with are
    <strong>Porto</strong>, where she died, and <strong>Aveiro</strong>, where the compound surname
    actually turns up.
  </p>

  <h2>The passport registers, worked</h2>
  <p>
    An item-level Portuguese passport register is a very good record. It carries the applicant's
    name, <strong>his parents</strong>, his age, trade and marital status, his parish and district,
    <strong>who travelled with him</strong>, and <strong>where he was going</strong>. For a family
    that left for Mozambique before 1924 it is close to the ideal document.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Porto is a dead end, and it was this archive's own idea <span class="tag disp">Closed</span></h3>
    <p>
      Porto was proposed because Ivone died there. But the surviving catalogued series —
      <strong>PT/ADPRT/AC/GCPRT/J-E/205, <em>Relação de passaportes emitidos pelo Governo Civil do
      Porto</em></strong> — runs <strong>1951 to 1991</strong>, is described at series level only
      rather than person by person, and has <strong>nothing digitised</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Half a century too late, unindexed, and offline. <strong>There is no Porto passport search to
      do for this period.</strong> Written down so nobody spends a weekend discovering it again.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Aveiro is indexed, and gave up one name</h2>
  <p>
    Aveiro's register is catalogued item by item with the full field set. Searched across every
    Portuguese archive on Digitarq for a Figueiredo bound for Lourenço Marques, the hits before 1924
    number exactly one.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>22 October 1904 <span class="tag inf">Candidate only — not evidence</span></h3>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Augusto Pereira de Figueiredo</strong>, de <strong>21 anos</strong> de idade, filho de
      <strong>Tiago Rodrigues de Figueiredo</strong> e de <strong>Maria Simões</strong>, da freguesia
      de <strong>Eixo</strong>, concelho de <strong>Aveiro</strong>. Passaporte para
      <strong>Lourenço Marques</strong>.
      <br /><span class="small">— PT/ADAVR/AC/GCAVR/H-D/001/0016/000610</span>
    </blockquote>
    <p><strong>For it:</strong> born c. 1883, so 41 when Ivone was born — ordinary. The right
    surname, which is what <em>Mota Figueiredo</em> requires of her father. Bound for Lourenço
    Marques itself, not merely Mozambique. The only such record in the indexed corpus. And Aveiro is
    also the one district where the compound name surfaces at all — a 1964 passport for
    <em>Arménio de Figueiredo e Maria Sofia <strong>Mota de Figueiredo</strong></em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Against it:</strong> Figueiredo is among the commonest surnames in Portugal, which on
    its own makes this nearly worthless. His mother was a Simões, so nothing here touches the Mota
    half. Nothing places him anywhere after 1904. And the index is a fraction of the whole —
    Digitarq holds 343,453 passport records but item-level personal indexing is wildly uneven
    between districts, with Porto absent entirely. <strong>“The only one” means “the only one in the
    indexed fraction”</strong>, and no more.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>On the Mota side, nothing.</strong> Sixty-two records mention Mota with Lourenço Marques
    and the only passport documents among them are Évora municipal lists of the 1840s and 50s and a
    Braga passport of 1898 — a century and a generation from what is wanted.
  </p>
  <h2>The Eixo registers, worked — and Augusto demoted</h2>
  <p>
    The Eixo parish fond (PT/ADAVR/PAVR04, 1590–1911) is digitised and openly licensed, in three
    series — baptisms, marriages, burials. <strong>Batismos 1883 is 51 images</strong>, the year
    Augusto was born. It is <strong>not name-indexed</strong>: for 1883 there is the book and nothing
    else. The hand is legible and the left margin carries each child's name, so reading it is slow
    rather than impossible.
  </p>
  <p>
    Faster: <strong>FamilySearch indexes Aveiro</strong> — Catholic Church Records 1550–1957 and
    Passport Registers 1882–1965. A search on the father's name across every FamilySearch Portugal
    collection returns <strong>five records in all</strong>, four of them this family.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Born</th><th>Mother</th><th>Went to</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Clemente Rodrigues de Figueiredo — <strong>died 25 April 1877</strong></td><td>1874</td><td>Maria Dias Pereira</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clemente Augusto de Figueiredo — passport 1912</td><td>1879, Eixo</td><td>Maria Dias Pereira</td><td><strong>Brazil</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clemente Augusto Figueiredo — passport 1920</td><td>1879, Eixo</td><td>Maria Dias Pereira</td><td><strong>Brazil</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Augusto Pereira de Figueiredo</strong> — passport 1904</td><td>1883, Eixo</td><td><strong>Maria Simões</strong></td><td><strong>Lourenço Marques</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    The household is real, and one detail of it is quietly awful: Thiago Rodrigues de Figueiredo had a
    son <strong>Clemente born in 1874 who died at three</strong>, in April 1877 — and the son born in
    1879 was given the dead child's name. Ordinary practice, and still hard to read.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Augusto is recorded to a different mother</strong> — Maria Simões, not Maria Dias Pereira —
    and two independent indexes read it the same way, so it is not one cataloguer's slip. The natural
    reading is that Thiago was widowed and remarried, making the two men half-brothers. Against that,
    Augusto's own name carries <em>Pereira</em>, the first wife's surname. Only the 1883 book can
    settle it.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the candidacy collapses <span class="tag disp">Demoted</span></h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No Figueiredo–Mota connection exists in Eixo or Aveiro.</strong> Digitarq returns <strong>zero</strong>. That was the one thing the hypothesis actually required.</li>
      <li><strong>No marriage for Augusto is indexed anywhere</strong> — in a district whose Catholic registers are indexed from 1550 to 1957. Had he come home to marry, it should be there.</li>
      <li><strong>His brother's two passports both went to Brazil</strong>, which is where Aveiro emigration overwhelmingly went. Lourenço Marques was the outlier.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing places Augusto anywhere after 22 October 1904.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      He is a real man from a real family who sailed for the right city at the right time, and there is
      nothing whatever tying him to a Mota or to Ivone. The one thing that recommended him — being the
      only indexed Figueiredo bound for Lourenço Marques before 1924 — is a fact about the
      <em>index</em>, not about the world.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      That was where this page stood before the book was read. It has since been read, and it changed
      the shape of the thing — see below.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The book, read</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>PT/ADAVR/PAVR04/1/32, <em>Batismos</em> 1883, image 31 of 51, entry Nº 35.</strong> About
    forty baptisms that year, two to a page. The left margin carries only the child's <em>given</em>
    name, so no surname sweep is possible — every entry has to be read. Augusto is the thirty-fifth
    child baptised in the parish that year.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Aos <strong>dezanove dias do mez d'Agosto</strong> do anno de mil oito centos oitenta e tres,
    nesta Igreja parochial de <strong>Santo Izidoro d'Eixo</strong>, Concelho d'Aveiro, Diocese de
    Coimbra, baptisei solemnemente um individuo do sexo masculino, a quem dei o nome de
    <strong>Augusto</strong>, o qual nasceu nesta freguezia … <strong>no dia doze do dito mez e
    anno</strong>, filho legitimo de <strong>Thiago Rodrigues de Figueiredo</strong> e
    <strong>Maria Simões Ferreira</strong>, <em>lavradores</em>, naturaes desta freguezia … neto
    paterno de <strong>Manoel Marques de Figueiredo</strong> e <strong>Sebastiana Marques
    Jacarina</strong>; materno de <strong>Ricardo Simões Ferreira</strong> e <strong>Joanna Dias
    Ferreira</strong>. Foram padrinhos <strong>João Nunes de Carvalho e Silva Junior</strong>,
    solteiro, negociante, e <strong>Florinda Simões Ferreira</strong>, solteira … só assignou o
    padrinho, porque a madrinha não sabia escrever.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>Born 12 August 1883, baptised a week later.</strong> His parents were farm labourers,
    both born in the parish. The godmother could not sign her name. And the entry hands over
    <strong>four grandparents</strong> — two generations further back than anything else this archive
    holds on the Portuguese side. <span class="tag doc">Documented</span>
  </p>

  <h2>What reading it settled</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Augusto and Clemente were half-brothers</h3>
    <p>
      Augusto's mother is <strong>Maria Simões Ferreira</strong>. Clemente Augusto, and the infant
      Clemente who died in 1877, are both recorded to <strong>Maria Dias Pereira</strong>. Different
      women. Thiago married twice and Augusto belongs to the second family — which is why he sailed
      for a different continent than his brother.
    </p>
    <p>
      And <em>“Pereira”</em> in his own indexed name is almost certainly a slip for
      <strong>Ferreira</strong>. Three Ferreiras appear in the entry — mother, maternal grandfather,
      godmother — and no Pereira at all. Portuguese naming makes him <strong>Augusto Ferreira de
      Figueiredo</strong>: mother's surname, then father's. <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And it opened the test that will finish him <span class="tag doc">The lead</span></h3>
    <p>A later hand has written down the margin of his baptism:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Falleceu no dia 21 de Agosto findo, na freguesia da Amadora, concelho de Oeiras.
      Reg. nº 247, livro 46 … Conservatória daquele concelho. … Aveiro, 8 de Setembro de 1954.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      He came back from Africa. <strong>Augusto died at Amadora, outside Lisbon, on 21 August
      1954</strong> — nine days after his seventy-first birthday — and the parish was told a
      fortnight later.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A Portuguese death record names the surviving spouse.</strong> So one document decides
      everything: <strong>Conservatória do Registo Civil de Oeiras, óbito, registo nº 247, livro 46,
      1954</strong>. If his widow was a <strong>Mota</strong>, he is Ivone's father and this closes
      with a name. If she was not, he is finished and it cost one request.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      An hour ago this was a man with a common surname on the right boat. He is now a man with a
      birth date, four grandparents, a death date, a parish, and a citable registration number.
      That is what reading the book bought.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Looked for, and not there</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No digitised <em>Boletim Oficial de Moçambique</em> for the 1920s–40s could be found. The Imprensa Nacional publishes only the modern <em>Boletim da República</em>.</li>
    <li>No online index of Lourenço Marques <strong>Catholic</strong> parish registers anywhere.</li>
    <li>MyHeritage holds no Portuguese or colonial civil registration at all — nine results for <em>Ivone Figueiredo</em>, eight Brazilian and one Floridian.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In order of likely return</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>AHM Maputo</strong>, through the online portal — her 1924 birth certificate, naming her parents.</li>
    <li><strong>Conservatória dos Registos Centrais</strong>, Lisbon — Henrique's 1941 birth, naming his father.</li>
    <li><s>Digitarq passport registers, Porto and Aveiro</s> — <strong>done</strong>. Porto closed as a dead end; Aveiro produced one candidate, above.</li>
    <li><s>The Eixo parish registers, for a Figueiredo–Mota marriage</s> — <strong>done. There is no such marriage</strong>, in Eixo or in the district.</li>
    <li><strong>AHU RNPAC</strong>, for a repatriation file or a passenger list, 1933–49.</li>
    <li><strong>The 1951 Lourenço Marques telephone book</strong>, in person at a FamilySearch Center.</li>
  </ol>
</Base>
